description:
FILED s533c (Opus, 2026-11-04), found while lowering #4185 (scratch/s533c/p/g1.pl), PRE-EXISTING (main 0f2db817).

REPRODUCER:
  no strict 'refs';
  my $caller = "main";
  our ($x, $y) = ("x0", "y0");
  (*{"${caller}::x"}, *{"${caller}::y"}) = (\"x1", \"y1");
  print "$x $y\n";
  (*x, *y) = (\"x2", \"y2");
  print "$x $y\n";
perl 5.40.3: x1 y1 / x2 y2
PCL: x0 y0 / x0 y0   (SILENT WRONG: nothing is assigned)

The SCALAR spelling works: `*{"${caller}::x"} = \"x1"` emits (p-glob-assign-dynamic NAME VAL), `*y = \"y1"` (p-glob-assign "main" "y" VAL).

MECHANISM: the list emits (p-list-= (vector (p-dynamic-typeglob NAME) (p-make-typeglob "main" "y")) RHS); p-list-='s generic arm stores each element through (p-setf PLACE VAL), and p-setf (cl/pcl-runtime.lisp) has no arm for a typeglob place -- its default `(box-set PLACE VAL)` writes into the freshly made glob object, which nothing reads.

FIX SHAPE (not done here -- s533c's #4185 lowers the LOCAL list through the glob-local family instead, per element): two p-setf arms, `p-make-typeglob` -> (p-glob-assign PKG NAME VAL) and `p-dynamic-typeglob` -> (p-glob-assign-dynamic NAME VAL), the scalar spelling's own forms (rule 11).  Probe: the reproducer, `(*x) = \*y` (whole-glob alias), `($s, *g) = (1, \"g")`, list-context value of the assignment.
